  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1201 PM CDT THU OCT 29 2026\n\nWe were treated to another cool start this morning across Central\nAlabama with temps in the mid 40s to mid 50s. We look to warm up\nnicely again today with highs topping out in the mid 70s to low\n80s. Rain-free conditions are expected to continue through the\nend of the work week as we remain under the influence of a\nweakening upper level ridge and surface high pressure. Similar to\nthe last couple of nights, light winds and clear skies will\npromote patchy river fog development late tonight into early\nFriday morning.\n\nMoisture associated with Tropical Storm Vance will begin to surge\nover the mountainous terrain of Mexico into the Plains and\neventually the Southeast by the weekend. This will lead to\nincreased rain chances, generally Saturday evening through Sunday\nmorning. While the greatest chances look to come overnight, we\nwill carry low chances (20-30%) Saturday afternoon so keep that\nin mind before heading out for some trick-or-treating.\n\nWeak ridging early next week will lead to a few rain-free days\nbefore our next system arrives by mid week. There are several\ndetails we will need to work out over the coming days such as how\nfar south the upper level low moves and how quickly it tracks\nthrough the region. For now, we will carry a moderate (40-60%)\nchance of showers and storms as ample moisture will be in place\nahead of this system.\n\n95/Castillo",
